=== SURVEY ===
Web source PageHeaderSticky.tsx is a PURE PRESENTATIONAL component (it fetches nothing). It is an
IntersectionObserver-driven sticky bar dropped into a page after the hero card: it observes a target
element by `id={targetId}` and, once that target scrolls ABOVE the top of the scroll viewport, pins a
compressed summary bar at `top: topOffset`. Visibility is `setVisible(!entry.isIntersecting &&
scrolledPast)` where `scrolledPast = entry.boundingClientRect.top < 0` and the observer uses
`rootMargin: -topOffset 0 0 0` + `threshold: 0`. `if (!visible) return null`. By default (`scrollToTop`)
the whole bar is a click-to-scroll-to-top button with a small `<ArrowUp>` glyph and aria-label
`${ariaLabel} — scroll to top`, scrolling the real scroll container `#main-content` smoothly to the top;
`scrollToTop={false}` renders a plain `role="region"` bar. It owns NO scroll container of its own.
Data sources (P1/S8): NONE — pure presentational (prompt's own "Data sources" section says so).
Shared components used: NONE. Charts/maps: NONE. i18n keys extracted from source: NONE (the component
is anonymous; each page passes its own already-localized `ariaLabel` + summary `children`; the only
copy it composes is the " — scroll to top" suffix on the button's aria-label).
Existing native: no PageHeaderSticky* symbols exist (grep = 0) → no duplicate-symbol risk.
Canonical surface pattern studied + mirrored: the closest committed analogue is the just-landed
shared-surfaces/BreadcrumbOverridesContext.* (also a pure, data-less layout primitive: Adapter /
Projection / Model / .swift / Views / Previews / strings / Tests / AdapterTests).

=== REASONING ===
- The web source is a presentational scroll-observer primitive. SwiftUI's idiomatic mapping of
  "show a sticky header once the hero scrolls out of view" is a view modifier on the page's ScrollView +
  a target marker, with the bar rendered through `safeAreaInset(edge: .top)` (the canonical SwiftUI
  `position: sticky`). Chosen architecture mirrors BreadcrumbOverridesContext:
  • Adapter (pure, Foundation): surface slug; PageHeaderStickyConfig (the props value type — topOffset
    clamps non-negative, web `top: topOffset`; composes the `${ariaLabel} — scroll to top` a11y label
    through an injected localizer); PageHeaderStickyGeometry (the IntersectionObserver entry: target top +
    bottom in viewport coords + viewport height); PageHeaderStickyVisibility (the VERBATIM port of the
    observer callback: isIntersecting over the top-inset root, scrolledPast = top < 0, visible =
    `!isIntersecting && scrolledPast`). No SwiftUI; every branch unit-testable.
  • Projection (pure): PageHeaderStickyPresentation (isVisible / mode scrollToTop|plain / regionLabel /
    scrollToTopLabel / topOffset / testID) + resolve(config:geometry:localize:) — the cached → projection
    adapter the acceptance calls for (web per-tick `visible` + the `scrollToTop ? <button> : <div>` split).
  • Model: PageHeaderStickyModel (@MainActor @Observable — the peer of `useState(visible)` + the
    observer effect: takes scroll geometry, derives the presentation through the pure projection, publishes
    only on change so a tick that does not flip visibility invalidates no observer = React re-render-only-
    when-`visible`-changes, emits view.opened once); Strings i18n facade; Telemetry seam + OSLog default.
  • .swift: PageHeaderStickyModifier (applied to a ScrollView — wires the anchor frame via a PreferenceKey
    over .scrollView(axis:) coordinate space + the viewport height via onScrollGeometryChange into the
    model, renders the bar in a top safeAreaInset, scroll-to-top via ScrollPosition.scrollTo(edge: .top)
    reduce-motion aware); .pageHeaderStickyTarget() (= web `id={targetId}`); .pageHeaderSticky(...) ergonomic
    entry + a model-injecting overload for previews/tests.
  • Views: PageHeaderStickyBar (= the rendered sticky div/button: backdrop-blurred glass surface, bottom
    hairline, single-line truncating summary row, trailing arrow.up glyph when interactive, full-width
    button with the composed a11y label vs. a labeled plain region) + DEBUG inspector staging EVERY real
    branch; Previews per branch; strings table; Tests + AdapterTests.
- States rendered: the source has NO loading/empty/error/stale/offline (it performs no fetch and reads no
  remote data — only a scroll position), so inventing that chrome would CONTRADICT the spec (covenant 5).
  The prompt's generic state list is explicitly qualified "(plus any state-specific branches that exist in
  the web source — read it and reproduce them all)"; the web source's REAL branches are reproduced + each
  rendered visibly (never a blank box): a live scroll demo that reveals the bar (the hidden ⇄ visible
  transition), the scroll-to-top button bar (arrow glyph), the plain bar (no arrow, labeled region), the
  long-summary single-line truncation, and a friendly explanatory note for the hidden branch where the web
  renderer shows nothing. The production modifier self-suppresses (renders no inset content) exactly like
  the web `if (!visible) return null`.
- Native adaptation (documented, not a shortcut): web connects bar↔hero by a global DOM id +
  IntersectionObserver; native connects them STRUCTURALLY via a PreferenceKey inside the same ScrollView
  the modifier wraps. web `position: sticky` ⇒ `safeAreaInset(edge: .top)`. web `#main-content` smooth
  scroll ⇒ ScrollPosition.scrollTo(edge: .top). web `<ArrowUp>` ⇒ SF Symbol arrow.up. The data, the
  visibility rule (incl. the topOffset rootMargin + the scrolledPast false-positive guard), the
  scroll-to-top affordance, the truncation, and the region/button accessibility are reproduced exactly.
- Rejected: porting Tailwind/hex literally (chrome is token-driven); inventing data-state chrome (source
  has none); HTTP in the view (none — purely in-process, like the web component).

